1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,900
The Science of Fever: How the Body Fights Infection.

2
00:00:03,850 --> 00:00:14,475
Fever is commonly viewed as a symptom of illness, but research increasingly reveals it as an active immunological signal — the body's deliberate strategy for fighting infection.

3
00:00:14,475 --> 00:00:25,750
When pathogens invade, immune cells release pyrogenic cytokines such as interleukin-1 (IL-1), interleukin-6 (IL-6), and tumor necrosis factor-alpha.

4
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:37,350
These molecules travel to the hypothalamus, the brain's temperature-regulating center, where they trigger prostaglandin E2 production, raising core temperature by 1 to 4 degrees Celsius.

5
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:46,037
A 2025 review in Frontiers in Cellular and Infection Microbiology described how elevated temperature impairs pathogen replication.

6
00:00:46,037 --> 00:00:57,150
Poliovirus replication declines 200-fold at 40 to 41 degrees Celsius, and febrile temperatures increase Gram-negative bacteria's susceptibility to immune attack.

7
00:00:57,150 --> 00:01:14,612
Research in Science Advances (January 2026) demonstrated that 39-degree Celsius temperatures cause influenza A virus to produce abnormal mini viral RNA molecules, activating the RIG-I signaling pathway and amplifying antiviral interferon production.

8
00:01:14,612 --> 00:01:22,950
A 2025 study in Trends in Immunology found that fever-range heat selectively boosts helper T cells (TH1 cells).

9
00:01:22,950 --> 00:01:35,987
At 39 degrees Celsius, mitochondrial complex I activity decreases in these cells, generating reactive oxygen species that activate the cGAS-STING pathway, increasing production of interferon-gamma.

10
00:01:35,987 --> 00:01:43,437
A 2025 study in Developmental Cell identified the motor protein Myosin II as a critical temperature sensor.

11
00:01:43,437 --> 00:01:53,087
When temperature rises, Myosin II increases mechanical force, propelling immune cells up to ten times faster toward infection sites within seconds.

12
00:01:53,087 --> 00:01:59,400
Fever temperatures above 40 degrees Celsius can cause tissue damage and require medical attention.

13
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:05,162
Understanding fever as an active immune process continues to reshape clinical approaches.

